Agent Response: Thanks — I can help you prepare. A few quick clarifying questions before I make specific recommendations:

- Are you currently taking any anticoagulant (warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban)? If warfarin, what is your target INR and when was that “last INR” measured? (You wrote “110” — do you mean 1.10 or something else?)
- Do you have diabetes or chronic kidney disease? Any recent lab results (A1c, creatinine, potassium)?
- Are you having any GI symptoms (blood in stool, black/tarry stools, change in bowel habits, unexplained weight loss, abdominal pain)?
- When was your last colonoscopy (if any) and ophthalmology visit for retinopathy?
- What is your current prednisone dose and how long is the taper scheduled for? (Steroids can raise BP and glucose.)

Important safety note: if your INR really is 110 (or even >10), that’s a medical emergency — call your provider or go to the emergency department right away because of very high bleeding risk. If you meant 1.10, that’s fine to clarify.

What I can suggest now (general information to discuss with Dr. Jennifer Lee):

Key topics to cover with your PCP about hypertension management
- BP target: Ask what target she recommends for you given age 52, atrial fibrillation, and retinopathy. (Common individualized targets: <130/80 for many people with cardiovascular risk or end-organ disease, but your clinician will tailor this.)
- Current control: Share your home BP readings (bring log or show readings from home monitor). Ask whether current meds (amlodipine 5 mg + lisinopril 10 mg) are adequate or need dose adjustment or an additional agent.
- Medication choices: Discuss whether an ACE inhibitor + CCB remains best for you. Because you have AFib, ask whether a beta-blocker or a nondihydropyridine CCB (diltiazem/verapamil) is indicated for rate control if needed — but those have different interactions and effects than amlodipine.
- Interaction and side-effect check: Review interactions with any anticoagulant or antiarrhythmic drugs; check kidney function and potassium due to lisinopril; ask whether prednisone taper is affecting BP or glucose.
- Sleep apnea: Confirm CPAP adherence and ask whether treating sleep apnea better could reduce BP and AF burden.
- Lifestyle and monitoring: Home BP monitoring schedule, dietary sodium, alcohol, exercise, weight loss. Ask if ambulatory BP monitoring is warranted if white-coat or masked hypertension suspected.
- Follow-up plan: How often to check BP and labs after any med change (BMP for ACEi, potassium), and when to return for medication adjustments.

Questions to ask about anticoagulation and pre-procedure planning
- If you are on warfarin: confirm target INR, when it was last checked, and whether it is within range. Ask how to manage warfarin if a procedure (colonoscopy) is planned (stop warfarin X days before; bridging decisions depend on stroke risk).
- If on a DOAC: ask when to hold before a procedure (usually 48–72 hours depending on renal function and bleeding risk) and when to resume.
- For any GI procedure: ask if a hematology consult or specific bridging plan is recommended given your AFib and thromboembolic risk.

About a referral to Boston GI (is it for colonoscopy or another GI concern?)
- Colon cancer screening: At age 52, guideline recommendations are to have regular colorectal cancer screening (started at 45 for average risk). If you have NOT had a recent colonoscopy (within 10 years) or stool testing as recommended, ask Dr. Lee to refer you to GI for screening colonoscopy.
- Increased-risk indications for earlier or expedited referral: family history of colorectal cancer, prior polyps, iron-deficiency anemia, GI bleeding, change in bowel habits, or inflammatory bowel disease. Tell your PCP these symptoms if present.
- If you are on anticoagulation, explicitly request the referral include pre-procedure anticoagulation instructions and contact between GI & your cardiology/primary team to coordinate stopping/bridging anticoagulation.
- If you have GI symptoms (bleeding, anemia), ask for expedited referral and possibly baseline labs (CBC, iron studies), and FIT test if appropriate.

Suggested labs and tests to ask for at your annual physical
- Basic metabolic panel (kidney function, electrolytes) — important on lisinopril.
- CBC (check for anemia — especially before GI workup).
- A1c (if you have diabetes history or retinopathy suggests prior diabetes).
- Lipid panel.
- Thyroid studies (TSH) — thyroid dysfunction can affect AFib.
- ECG (to document rhythm) and consider echocardiogram if not done recently.
- If on warfarin: INR check and confirm target range and stability.
- Urine albumin/creatinine ratio if diabetes or hypertension and kidney risk.

Practical phrasing you can use with Dr. Lee
- “Given my AFib, retinopathy, and current meds (amlodipine 5 mg, lisinopril 10 mg), what BP target should I aim for and are these meds adequate?”
- “Am I due for colorectal cancer screening? If so, can you refer me to GI (Boston GI) and please coordinate anticoagulation management for the procedure?”
- “Can we review my anticoagulation: which agent I’m on, my most recent INR (please confirm the number), and a plan if I need any invasive procedures?”
- “Please check BMP, CBC, A1c, lipids, and TSH today and let me know if any med changes are needed.”

Pre-visit checklist for you
- Bring an up-to-date medication list (including steroids, doses).
- Bring recent home BP logs and any recent lab results.
- Note any GI symptoms, bleeding, or change in bowel habits.
- Know whether you’re on warfarin vs a DOAC and the date of last INR or anticoagulant dosing.
- Make sure ophthalmology for retinopathy is up to date.

If you want, I can:
- Draft an email or message to Dr. Jennifer Lee summarizing these questions and requesting a referral to Boston GI (or GI referral wording for colonoscopy), and include coordination for anticoagulation — would you like that? If so, tell me whether you want me to send it now or save as a draft and confirm any additional details (anticoagulant name, correct INR reading, any GI symptoms).

Finally: please confirm the INR value and whether you’re on warfarin — if that 110 is accurate, seek immediate medical attention.
